 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files still
+  only warn and end in `exit 0`.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, or swallowing internal errors so the script exits 0 after one of its own commands failed.
+- Reason: maintainer decision. Failing open hid breakage inside the check, so nobody found out when it stopped working.
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open.
+- Note: under `-e`, a body ending in `test && cmd` returns non-zero when the test is false and aborts the check. Use
+  `if … then … fi`.
+
+### §check-fails-open — superseded 2026-10-22 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
